---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ToolPanel from '../../components/ToolPanel.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Convert GPX to FIT in your browser"
  description="Turn a recorded GPX track into a FIT activity file with distance, laps and a session summary. Runs locally in your browser."
  schema={{
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'GPX to FIT converter',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Any (web browser)',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  }}
>
  <h1>Convert GPX to FIT</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Turn a recorded GPX track into a FIT activity. Pick the activity type first, since GPX does not record it in a
    standard way.
  </p>

  <ToolPanel tool="gpx2fit" />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What the FIT file contains</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>One record per track point: time, position, elevation, and heart rate, cadence, temperature and power where the GPX has them.</li>
      <li>Distance, calculated point to point from the positions, because GPX does not store it.</li>
      <li>One lap and one session covering the whole track, with totals and averages.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>This makes an activity, not a course</h2>
    <p>
      The output is a record of something that happened. That needs a time on every point. A route drawn in a planner
      has positions but no times, and the tool will refuse it with that explanation. It does not write FIT course
      files for following a route on a device.
    </p>
    <p>
      Speed is not written per point; software that needs it derives it from distance and time. Pauses are not
      detected, so moving time equals elapsed time.
    </p>
    <p>Going the other way is <a href="/fit-to-gpx/">FIT to GPX</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Layout>
